 "rule": {
  "name": "two_qubit_pauli_via_dual_cd_loop_on_home_mode",
  "kind": "synth",
  "contract": "exact",
  "idea": "exp(-i theta P_j P_k) for a two-qubit Pauli string with the SAME Pauli on both qubits (XX, YY or ZZ) is built from a single shared oscillator mode m via two native conditional-displacement (CD) gates per qubit, instead of a Pauli-string-conditioned CD bus built from CP conjugations. Rotate P -> Z with a one-qubit basis change (H for X; H.Sdg for Y; identity for Z), then apply A = CD(beta, j, m), B = CD(gamma, k, m), A^-1, B^-1 with beta = +-sqrt(|theta|/2), gamma = i*sqrt(|theta|/2) (sign of beta flips with the sign of theta). Since [Z_j(beta a^+ - beta* a), Z_k(gamma a^+ - gamma* a)] = (beta gamma* - beta* gamma) Z_j Z_k is a c-number times Z_j Z_k, it commutes with both generators, BCH truncates exactly, and e^A e^B e^{-A} e^{-B} = exp(2i Im(beta gamma*) Z_j Z_k) = exp(-i theta Z_j Z_k), returning the mode to whatever state it started in (vacuum ancilla or an already-populated mode). Undo the basis change to finish. Only 4 two-op CD gates (plus O(1) one-qubit gates) are needed, versus the ~8 CP + 2 D construction of the general Pauli-string bus rule.",
  "term_kinds": [
   "pauli"
  ],
  "stage": "logical"
 },
